  "body": "Part of #361. Found while making lathe holder bodies reachable from the tool editor and MCP.\n\n**What happens.** A turning tool can carry a lathe holder body (#369), and every move is judged against the part and the standing stock (`operation/turning_holder.rs`: \"What stands. The part, always, and the stock less every cut\"). The setup's workholding and fixtures are not in that reading. For a turning operation the fixture laws (`workholding_warnings` in `operation/generate.rs`) still sweep the tool record's holder cylinder (`tool_body::judged` returns the record unchanged for a lathe-frame body), and a turning insert's holder cylinder is normally 0 x 0. So an offset holder that runs into the chuck jaws, a tailstock or a steady rest passes with no finding - while the operation no longer carries the \"holder not judged\" note, because a body is present.\n\n**Why it matters.** A user who imported the vendor's holder model reads \"judged\" as \"judged against everything\". A holder striking the chuck is a crash.\n\n**Fix.** Read the lathe body's clearance bands (`camcraft_tools::lathe_holder::LatheClearance`) against the setup's steel as well: workholding that turns with the part as its revolved envelope (same reading as the stock), standing steel (tailstock, steady rest) in the machine frame. Until a kind of steel can be read, say so by name on the operation (a note like the existing \"holder not judged\"), never silently.\n\n**Done means:** an offset lathe holder that reaches a chuck-jaw fixture is a blocking finding naming the move; the same holder clear of it passes; steel the law cannot read is named in a note. Pinned at the app level.\n",
